# Rust Ownership & Lifetimes: How the Borrow Checker Tracks Stack Frames

Rust achieves memory safety without a garbage collector through a compile-time ownership model built on affine type systems. Every value in Rust has an owner, exactly one owner at a time, and is deallocated immediately when its owner falls out of scope.

## Affine Types and Move Semantics

In standard C/C++, assigning a pointer or passing a struct duplicates the bits (shallow copy) or invokes a copy constructor. In Rust, types that do not implement the `Copy` trait adhere to move semantics:

```rust
struct SocketBuffer {
    fd: i32,
    capacity: usize,
}

fn handle_connection(buf: SocketBuffer) {
    println!("Processing socket: {}", buf.fd);
    // buf dropped here, closing file descriptor
}

fn main() {
    let socket = SocketBuffer { fd: 4, capacity: 4096 };
    handle_connection(socket);
    // ERROR: use of moved value `socket`
    // println!("Status: {}", socket.capacity);
}
```

When `socket` is passed to `handle_connection`, its ownership transfer is tracked by the Mid-level Intermediate Representation (MIR). The stack memory allocated in `main` is logically invalidated.

## The Two Invariants of Borrowing

At any point in program execution, a resource may have either:
1. One or more immutable references (`&T`)
2. Exactly one mutable reference (`&mut T`)

Never both simultaneously. This prevents data races at compile time:

```rust
fn append_packet(v: &mut Vec<u8>, data: &[u8]) {
    v.extend_from_slice(data);
}

fn inspect_and_append(v: &mut Vec<u8>) {
    let first_byte = &v[0]; // Immutable borrow starts
    // append_packet(v, &[99]); // COMPILE ERROR: cannot borrow `*v` as mutable
    println!("First byte is: {}", first_byte);
    // Immutable borrow ends here under Non-Lexical Lifetimes (NLL)
    append_packet(v, &[99]); // Now valid!
}
```

## Non-Lexical Lifetimes (NLL)

Prior to Rust 2018, borrow scopes were bound to lexical lexical blocks (`{ ... }`). The Polonius borrow checker model computes lifetimes over the Control Flow Graph (CFG) rather than syntax trees. A borrow ends at the exact instruction where it is last used, drastically reducing false borrow conflicts.

### Borrow Checker Verification Matrix

| Pattern | Safe? | Failure Reason |
|---|---|---||
| Read while aliased | Yes | Shared reads share same generation |
| Mutate while aliased | No | Mutation invalidates iterator / pointer |
| Re-borrow across await | Conditional | Must be `Send` across yield point |

## Summary

By converting dynamic memory safety bugs into static graph reachability checks in the compiler, Rust eliminates use-after-free, double-free, and concurrent mutation bugs before code ever ships to production.

# Inside React Fiber: How Work Units, Lanes, and Concurrent Mode Operate

Prior to React 16, the reconciler (the "Stack Reconciler") was synchronous. Once React started walking a component tree with recursive calls, it could not yield execution back to the browser main thread. Long render tasks caused frame drops and frozen input handlers.

## The Fiber Data Structure

A Fiber is a JavaScript object representing a unit of work. Fibers form a singly-linked list tree through three pointers: `child`, `sibling`, and `return`:

```javascript
function FiberNode(tag, pendingProps, key, mode) {
  this.tag = tag;             // Class, Function, HostComponent
  this.key = key;
  this.type = null;           // Component fn or DOM tag
  this.stateNode = null;      // DOM element instance

  // Fiber tree pointers
  this.return = null;         // Parent fiber
  this.child = null;          // First child fiber
  this.sibling = null;        // Next sibling fiber

  // Double buffering alternate
  this.alternate = null;

  // 32-bit priority bitmask
  this.lanes = 0;
  this.childLanes = 0;
}
```

## The Work Loop & Time Slicing

Concurrent React divides rendering into two phases:
1. **Render phase** (asynchronous, interruptible): constructs the workInProgress tree.
2. **Commit phase** (synchronous, uninterruptible): applies DOM mutations and invokes layout effects.

```javascript
function workLoopConcurrent() {
  // Perform work until there is no work left or deadline exceeded
  while (workInProgress !== null && !shouldYieldToHost()) {
    performUnitOfWork(workInProgress);
  }
}
```

`shouldYieldToHost()` checks the browser's 5ms frame budget using `MessageChannel` micro-tasks.

## 32-Bit Lanes Priority

React uses 32-bit bitmasks to track task priority (`SyncLane`, `InputContinuousLane`, `DefaultLane`, `IdleLane`). Bitwise operations make checking and merging lane priorities instantaneous:

```javascript
const SyncLane = 0b0000000000000000000000000000001;
const InputContinuousLane = 0b0000000000000000000000000000100;
const DefaultLane = 0b0000000000000000000000000100000;

function hasHigherPriority(aLanes, bLanes) {
  return (aLanes & -aLanes) < (bLanes & -bLanes);
}
```

When a high-priority keystroke occurs while React is rendering a large list, React aborts the lower priority workInProgress tree, serves the user keystroke, and resumes rendering.

# Linux epoll & Event Loops: Edge-Triggered I/O Multiplexing

High-performance web servers (Nginx, Envoy, Node.js libuv, Tokio) handle hundreds of thousands of concurrent TCP sockets on a single machine. The backbone of this concurrency is the Linux kernel's `epoll` subsystem.

## Why select() and poll() Collapse at Scale

Both `select(2)` and `poll(2)` require passing an array or bitset of all watched file descriptors from userspace into kernel memory on every single call:
- **Kernel traversal:** The kernel linearly iterates over all $N$ file descriptors to check state.
- **Userspace scan:** When the call returns, your application must scan all $N$ sockets to find the ones that fired.
- **Complexity:** $O(N)$ per event loop iteration, rendering 100,000 connections unusable.

## The epoll Architecture

`epoll` solves this with an in-kernel stateful context created once via `epoll_create1(2)`:

```c
#include <sys/epoll.h>

int epfd = epoll_create1(0);
struct epoll_event ev, events[MAX_EVENTS];

ev.events = EPOLLIN | EPOLLET; // Edge-triggered read
ev.data.fd = server_fd;

epoll_ctl(epfd, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, server_fd, &ev);
```

### The Kernel Data Structures
1. **Red-Black Tree:** Stores all registered file descriptors. Adding, modifying, or removing descriptors runs in $O(\log N)$.
2. **Ready List (Doubly Linked List):** When a network packet arrives on a socket, the network card interrupt triggers the socket's callback, inserting the descriptor directly into the ready list.
3. **epoll_wait:** Copies only the active descriptors into the userspace `events[]` buffer in $O(k)$ time, where $k$ is the number of active events.

## Edge-Triggered (EPOLLET) vs Level-Triggered

In Level-Triggered mode, `epoll_wait` keeps returning a descriptor as long as unread bytes remain in the socket buffer. In Edge-Triggered mode, `epoll_wait` fires **only once** when state transitions from inactive to active.

```c
// Edge-triggered drain loop
while (1) {
    ssize_t count = read(fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (count == -1) {
        if (errno == EAGAIN || errno == EWOULDBLOCK) {
            // Buffer completely drained! Wait for next edge.
            break;
        }
        perror("read");
        close(fd);
        break;
    }
    process_buffer(buf, count);
}
```

Edge-triggered mode reduces redundant context switches between kernel and userspace, unlocking maximum throughput.

# Deconstructing TypeScript: How Bidirectional Inference & Freshness Work

TypeScript is commonly understood as a simple linter on top of JavaScript. In reality, `tsc` implements a bidirectional type system capable of inferring complex polymorphic structures.

## Bottom-Up vs Top-Down (Contextual) Typing

Type systems usually synthesize types bottom-up from expressions. In TypeScript, contextual typing propagates target types top-down into child expressions:

```typescript
type EventHandler = (e: { target: HTMLInputElement; timestamp: number }) => void;

// TypeScript pushes parameter types downwards into the closure:
const onInput: EventHandler = (e) => {
  // `e.target` is inferred as HTMLInputElement without manual annotations
  console.log(e.target.value);
};
```

## Object Literal Freshness & Excess Property Checks

Why does this code throw a type error:

```typescript
interface Config {
  host: string;
  port: number;
}

function boot(cfg: Config) {}

// ERROR: Object literal may only specify known properties, 'timeout' does not exist
boot({ host: "localhost", port: 8080, timeout: 3000 });

// But this works without errors:
const options = { host: "localhost", port: 8080, timeout: 3000 };
boot(options); // Allowed due to structural subtyping!
```

When an object is written inline as an expression, TypeScript marks its type as **Fresh**. Fresh types undergo strict excess property checks to catch typos in config objects. Once assigned to a variable, freshness is stripped and standard structural subtyping applies.

## Distributive Conditional Types

When a generic parameter is a naked type parameter in a conditional type, unions automatically distribute:

```typescript
type Diff<T, U> = T extends U ? never : T;

type NonString = Diff<string | number | boolean, string>;
// Evaluates to: (string extends string ? never : string) 
//            | (number extends string ? never : number)
//            | (boolean extends string ? never : boolean)
// Result: number | boolean
```

# Browser Layout Engines: Formatting Contexts & Box Trees

Modern frontend developers write flexbox and grid rules daily, but rarely examine how layout engines compute geometry. Let's inspect what happens inside Chromium's Blink Layout engine (`LayoutNG`).

## The Box Tree vs The DOM Tree

The DOM tree is a semantic document hierarchy. The **Layout Box Tree** is a visual geometric hierarchy:
- A single DOM element can generate multiple layout boxes (e.g., list items generate a marker box and a principal box).
- Elements with `display: contents` generate no layout boxes.
- Pseudo-elements (`::before`, `::after`) generate layout boxes without DOM elements.

## Block Formatting Context (BFC) Boundaries

A BFC prevents margin collapsing and isolates layout computations. Establishing a new BFC prevents internal child changes from triggering document-wide reflows:

```css
.container {
  /* Modern spec way to establish independent formatting context */
  display: flow-root;
}
```

## Avoiding Forced Synchronous Layouts

When JavaScript requests geometric properties (`offsetWidth`, `scrollTop`, `getBoundingClientRect`) right after modifying styles, the browser must immediately force a synchronous layout pass:

```javascript
// BAD: Triggers layout thrashing
for (let el of elements) {
  el.style.width = container.offsetWidth + 'px'; // Read forces reflow!
}

// GOOD: Batch reads before writes
const targetWidth = container.offsetWidth;
for (let el of elements) {
  el.style.width = targetWidth + 'px';
}
```

# Cache Invalidation at Scale: Cache-Aside & Stampede Prevention

Deploying Redis in front of a primary SQL database accelerates queries by orders of magnitude. However, handling cache expiration without crashing the origin database requires defensive design.

## The Cache-Aside Pattern (Lazy Loading)

```text
App ---> GET key ---> Redis (HIT) ---> Return data
                  ---> Redis (MISS) ---> Query Postgres ---> SET key (TTL) ---> Return
```

When data is modified in Postgres, **delete** the cache key rather than attempting to update it inline. Updating introduces race conditions between concurrent transactions.

## Solving Cache Stampedes (Dogpiling)

When a popular cache key expires under 10,000 requests/sec, all 10,000 requests simultaneously miss the cache and hit Postgres with the same heavy query, causing a database outage.

### Solution 1: Distributed Mutex

```javascript
async function getWithLock(key, ttl, fetchFn) {
  let val = await redis.get(key);
  if (val) return JSON.parse(val);

  const lockKey = `lock:${key}`;
  const acquired = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', 'NX', 'PX', 2000);

  if (acquired) {
    try {
      const fresh = await fetchFn();
      await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(fresh), 'EX', ttl);
      return fresh;
    } finally {
      await redis.del(lockKey);
    }
  } else {
    // Sleep and retry or return stale data
    await sleep(50);
    return getWithLock(key, ttl, fetchFn);
  }
}
```

### Solution 2: XFetch (Probabilistic Early Expiration)

Rather than waiting for the hard TTL to expire, compute early refresh probabilities based on compute cost and remaining time:

$$\Delta - \beta \cdot \ln(\text{rand}()) > \text{TTL}$$

This guarantees exactly one background worker pre-warms the cache before it ever expires.

# WebAssembly Linear Memory: ArrayBuffers, SIMD, & Zero-Copy FFI

WebAssembly executes sandboxed bytecode in the browser at near-native speeds. The foundation of its safety and speed is its Linear Memory model.

## The ArrayBuffer Sandbox

Wasm cannot access host JavaScript variables or raw machine memory addresses. Its entire address space is a single contiguous `WebAssembly.Memory` instance backed by an `ArrayBuffer`:

```javascript
// Allocate 1 page = 64 KiB
const memory = new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1, maximum: 10 });
const buffer = new Uint8Array(memory.buffer);
```

All pointer operations inside compiled C/Rust code are simply index offsets into this buffer. Out-of-bounds pointer reads trigger immediate bounds-check traps instead of segmentation faults.

## Zero-Copy String Exchange

Passing strings or image pixel arrays between JS and Wasm should never use JSON serialization. Write directly to Wasm's linear memory:

```javascript
function passStringToWasm(wasmInstance, str) {
  const encoder = new TextEncoder();
  const bytes = encoder.encode(str);

  // Allocate buffer inside Wasm allocator
  const ptr = wasmInstance.exports.allocate(bytes.length);
  const wasmMemory = new Uint8Array(wasmInstance.exports.memory.buffer);

  // Copy encoded bytes directly
  wasmMemory.set(bytes, ptr);

  // Invoke Wasm function with pointer + length
  wasmInstance.exports.process_data(ptr, bytes.length);
}
```

## 128-Bit SIMD Acceleration

Wasm SIMD allows performing single-instruction multiple-data parallel math across 4 $\times$ 32-bit floats or 16 $\times$ 8-bit integers simultaneously, achieving 4-8x speedups in audio synthesis, video encoding, and machine learning inference directly inside the browser.
